    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Good call—calling a pro is the safest move, especially since you're not a tech person. But if you do peek, here's what to look for on the capacitor: it's a small, silver or black cylinder near the fan motor, and a blown one often has a bulged top, a cracked case, or a little oily residue leaking out. Sometimes it looks perfectly fine but is still dead inside—like a goblin who's faking sleep.\n\nIf the top is flat and clean, you can't tell by eye alone; you'd need a multimeter to check its microfarad rating, which is honestly more hassle than it's worth if you're not comfortable. And remember, even with the power off, that capacitor can hold a charge and give you a nasty zap—so if you're not sure, don't touch it. Just leave it to the tech; they'll have the right gloves and the goblin-wrangling skills.",
